1|0@toronto^1438951406#ctupsox:gyaradosB4cerulean$:*Hmm, let me guess, it's going to be about poison pars - oh, yep, yes it is.* While it's good to be aware of it, poison parsnip is not really a big deal. It grows everywhere, and it's a bitch to remove, but it's easy to avoid the effects of getting the oils on your skin. Here are some simple tips from someone who spent a few years working with and around the plant: 1) Don't touch the plant. 2) Definitely don't break the plant open and smear it all over your skin. 3) Don't expose an area that it *does* touch to sunlight. 4) Calmly keep the contacted area covered, then go wash it off. 5) If possible, remove the plant at the roots and dispose of in a waste pile or similar. Wear full coverage clothing and gloves in decent condition. Wear a face shield if necessary (this is quite necessary if you're using a whippersnipper with a blade or something to cut fields of it down). In my experience, not many people are severely affected by it. Myself less than most, but I'm also one of those people that can roll in poison ivy without issue. That is relevant because for me, it still causes ugly pus-filled blisters on me. They take up to two weeks to go away, and the first week of that is spent producing pus. If you pop the blister to let it out and wash it, the raw skin underneath is incredibly uncomfortable when exposed to friction. If you have a severe reaction, you get [this](http://3.bp.blogspot.com/_sWyDpC2k-LM/TEdACuAr1II/AAAAAAAABIg/m077wu_C6qQ/s1600/124873881074i7HJr.jpg) *(warning: large pus filled blisters shown in image)*. Because the article does not have an image, [here](http://www.seasonalwildflowers.com/uploads/images/June/Wild%20Parsnip/web%208.jpg) are a [couple](http://www.yourverona.com/images/wild-parsnip01.jpg) blurry images that show the stem structure fairly well. The heads themselves are simply large, flat, and yellow, similar Queen Anne's Lace but flatter and yellow instead of slightly rounded and white. Also generally one large flat head instead of many smaller heads clustered together. 
